{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PdfDjLdGgALUUKZprUFPrD","type":"tool_result","content":"276\t- **Required figures (anchor stubs)**: `ch14-fig01-manifest-to-package-progression`, `ch14-fig02-helm-chart-anatomy`, `ch14-fig03-release-vs-chart-vs-revision`, `ch14-fig04-kustomize-base-overlay`, `ch14-zenith-package-not-template`\n277\t- **Question budget**: 8 Soundings · 10 Bearings · 17 Practice · **35 total**\n278\t- **Notes**: Blocking gap G19 (Kustomize). **[B3] Decay risk — thin downstream presence.** Named anchors are mandatory in **Ch 15** (Argo CD's manifest sources include Helm charts — the reason Helm is taught first) and **Ch 17** (CRDs shipped as chart content, feeding the operator/extensibility synthesis).\n279\t\n280\t---\n281\t\n282\t### Chapter 15 — The Chart Is the Truth\n283\t\n284\t- **Subtitle**: *\"GitOps is the control loop you already learned, pointed at a repository\"*\n285\t- **Purpose**: domain — D3.1 Cloud native application delivery / GitOps and delivery (chapter_type: `content`)\n286\t- **Covers**: **D3.1** — the twelve-factor app; deployment strategies (rolling, Recreate, blue/green, canary, A/B); CI/CD vs GitOps; the four OpenGitOps principles; Argo CD (controller model, Git as source of truth, OutOfSync, sync, tracking branches/tags/commits, PreSync/Sync/PostSync hooks, drift detection, rollback); Flux; multi-cluster delivery\n287\t- **Prerequisites**: Ch 3 (the control loop), Ch 6 (controllers, rolling update mechanics), Ch 14 (Helm charts as a manifest source)\n288\t- **Target length**: Content-driven, no word target. **Depth band: substantial** — 7 points, and it carries the book's primary Zenith\n289\t- **Retrieval targets**: **25% — ceiling** **[B3]**, from all previous. ≥4-back floor satisfied several times over. Mandatory anchors: **the control loop (Ch 3)** — the retrieval that *is* the Zenith; rolling-update mechanics (Ch 6) now given strategy vocabulary; Helm charts (Ch 14) as Argo's input; ConfigMap/Secret (Ch 4) as twelve-factor factor III; drift detection against `spec`/`status` (Ch 4)\n290\t- **Key concepts introduced**: The twelve factors that actually matter in Kubernetes; the strategy vocabulary (blue/green, canary, A/B) attached to Argo's sync hooks that give it mechanism; **push CI/CD vs pull GitOps**; the four OpenGitOps principles as a restatement of reconciliation; OutOfSync as `status ≠ spec` where spec lives in Git\n291\t- **Required figures (anchor stubs)**: `ch15-fig01-twelve-factor-in-kubernetes`, `ch15-fig02-deployment-strategies-compared`, `ch15-fig03-cicd-push-vs-gitops-pull`, `ch15-fig04-argocd-sync-states-and-hooks`, `ch15-fig05-opengitops-four-principles`, `ch15-zenith-control-loop-pointed-at-a-repo`\n292\t- **Question budget**: 8 Soundings · 10 Bearings · 21 Practice · **39 total**\n293\t- **Notes**: Blocking gaps G9 (deployment strategy vocabulary), G18 (Flux). **PRIMARY ZENITH.** `ch15-zenith` must visually re-present `ch03-fig02` with Git substituted for etcd as the desired-state store — the recognition is the payoff, and it fails if the two figures don't rhyme. This is the strongest synthesis opportunity in the book and the reason Part IV is ordered packaging → delivery → debugging.\n294\t\n295\t---\n296\t\n297\t### Chapter 16 — Your Application, Their Cluster\n298\t\n299\t- **Subtitle**: *\"Four questions that separate 'my code is broken' from 'the platform is broken'\"*\n300\t- **Purpose**: domain — D3.2 Cloud native application delivery / debugging, application scope (chapter_type: `content`)\n301\t- **Covers**: **D3.2** — the application-scope triage flow; `kubectl debug` and ephemeral containers; init-container debugging; `exec`; `port-forward`; debugging Services and StatefulSets from the application side; local development and debugging; the handoff boundary with Ch 13\n302\t- **Prerequisites**: Ch 5 (Pod, init containers, probes), Ch 9 (Service, DNS), Ch 13 (platform-scope triage, the handoff)\n303\t- **Target length**: Content-driven, no word target. **Depth band: focused** — 4 points, high retrieval load\n304\t- **Retrieval targets**: **25% — ceiling** **[B3]**, from all previous. Mandatory anchors: the Ch 13 handoff, retrieved as the chapter's opening move; Pod phases and probes (Ch 5); Service→EndpointSlice→Pod path (Ch 9) as the \"is my Service even selecting anything\" check; StatefulSet identity (Ch 6) and its PVC pairing (Ch 11)\n305\t- **Key concepts introduced**: The four triage questions; ephemeral containers and why you cannot just add a container to a running Pod; `port-forward` as a diagnostic that deliberately bypasses the Service path; the scope boundary — what is yours to fix and what is the platform's\n306\t- **Required figures (anchor stubs)**: `ch16-fig01-application-scope-triage`, `ch16-fig02-ephemeral-container-debug`, `ch16-fig03-portforward-vs-service-path`, `ch16-zenith-mine-or-the-platforms`\n307\t- **Question budget**: 8 Soundings · 10 Bearings · 15 Practice · **33 total**\n308\t- **Notes**: Blocking gaps G1, G2. Closes the two-chapter troubleshooting arc opened in Ch 13. **Build the reciprocal cross-bearings deliberately** — Ch 13 hands off, Ch 16 opens by handing back. B1 sequencing implication #7: the exam splits these across domains, the reader's mental model must not be split.\n309\t\n310\t---\n311\t\n312\t### Chapter 17 — The Fleet and Its Charts\n313\t\n314\t- **Subtitle**: *\"Meshes, functions, autoscalers, and the foundation that keeps the map\"*\n315\t- **Purpose**: domain — D4.2 + D4.3 Cloud native architecture, community and collaboration (chapter_type: `content`)\n316\t- **Covers**: **D4.2 + D4.3** — the CNCF cloud native definition v1.1 and its characteristics; loose coupling; microservices; immutable infrastructure; declarative APIs; **the extension-points synthesis** (CRI/CNI/CSI, CRDs, API aggregation, admission webhooks, device plugins, operators); service mesh (data vs control plane, Envoy, mTLS and zero trust, sidecar vs ambient); serverless and Knative (Serving, Eventing, Functions, scale to zero); the autoscaling landscape (VPA, KEDA, Cluster Autoscaler, Karpenter); CNCF maturity levels; the graduated roster as dated data; the CNCF Landscape; Governing Board / TOC / TAGs / End User TAB; Kubernetes SIGs / Working Groups / Committees / Steering; the contributor ladder; KEPs; release cadence; KubeCon; the Code of Conduct; the CNCF certification ladder\n317\t- **Prerequisites**: Ch 2 (CRI), Ch 6 (CRDs, operator pattern, HPA concept), Ch 9 (CNI), Ch 12 (mTLS, zero trust, policy engines), Ch 14 (charts as operator delivery), Ch 15 (declarative delivery)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c450c9f-3efb-4615-9d77-0943167fd975","uuid":"fbe82489-9f13-4ea8-8606-d00b82c1293b","timestamp":"2026-08-31T09:53:08.344Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\arc-outline.md","content":"- **Required figures (anchor stubs)**: `ch14-fig01-manifest-to-package-progression`, `ch14-fig02-helm-chart-anatomy`, `ch14-fig03-release-vs-chart-vs-revision`, `ch14-fig04-kustomize-base-overlay`, `ch14-zenith-package-not-template`\n- **Question budget**: 8 Soundings · 10 Bearings · 17 Practice · **35 total**\n- **Notes**: Blocking gap G19 (Kustomize). **[B3] Decay risk — thin downstream presence.** Named anchors are mandatory in **Ch 15** (Argo CD's manifest sources include Helm charts — the reason Helm is taught first) and **Ch 17** (CRDs shipped as chart content, feeding the operator/extensibility synthesis).\n\n---\n\n### Chapter 15 — The Chart Is the Truth\n\n- **Subtitle**: *\"GitOps is the control loop you already learned, pointed at a repository\"*\n- **Purpose**: domain — D3.1 Cloud native application delivery / GitOps and delivery (chapter_type: `content`)\n- **Covers**: **D3.1** — the twelve-factor app; deployment strategies (rolling, Recreate, blue/green, canary, A/B); CI/CD vs GitOps; the four OpenGitOps principles; Argo CD (co