{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TRLmKMpB5o5GJi262cKMo2","type":"tool_result","content":"580\t| 72 | Expecting `kubectl top` to work without metrics-server. | `kubectl top` and autoscaling depend on the **resource metrics pipeline** (metrics-server) collecting from kubelets. | [source] |\n581\t\n582\t### D3 — Cloud Native Application Delivery\n583\t\n584\t| # | Trap | Correct understanding | Tag |\n585\t|---|---|---|---|\n586\t| 73 | \"GitOps means running CI from Git.\" | GitOps is four specific principles about *desired state*: declarative, versioned and immutable, **pulled** automatically, continuously reconciled. CI is not part of the definition. | [source] |\n587\t| 74 | Missing \"**pulled**\" — assuming a pipeline pushes to the cluster. | Principle 3 is explicit: agents **pull** desired-state declarations from the source. Push-based CD is not GitOps. | [source] |\n588\t| 75 | Treating reconciliation as one-shot at deploy time. | Principle 4 is **continuous** observation and application, indefinitely. | [source] |\n589\t| 76 | \"OutOfSync means the sync failed.\" | OutOfSync means **live state deviates from the Git target state** — including because a human changed something in the cluster. It is a drift signal, not an error. | [source] |\n590\t| 77 | \"Argo CD only deploys plain YAML.\" | It supports Kustomize, Helm, Jsonnet, plain YAML/JSON directories, and custom config-management plugins. | [source] |\n591\t| 78 | \"Argo CD can only track a branch.\" | It can track branches, tags, or a **pinned Git commit**. | [source] |\n592\t| 79 | \"Helm is a templating engine.\" | Helm is a packaging system: chart (the package) → values (configuration) → templates (which render manifests) → **release** (an installed instance). | [source] |\n593\t| 80 | Confusing **chart** with **release**. | One chart can be installed many times; each install is a separately-named release, independently upgradable and rollback-able. | [source] |\n594\t| 81 | Confusing `charts/` with a chart repository. | `charts/` is a directory of **dependency** charts inside a chart. A chart repository is an **HTTP server** housing packaged charts, managed with `helm repo`. | [source] |\n595\t| 82 | \"Knative is a Kubernetes replacement.\" | Knative is Kubernetes-based, builds on the Pod abstraction, and is implemented as **CRDs**. | [source] |\n596\t| 83 | Confusing Knative Serving with Eventing. | Serving = HTTP-triggered autoscaling container runtime with scale-to-zero. Eventing = CloudEvents-over-HTTP **asynchronous** routing. | [source] |\n597\t| 84 | Treating \"serverless\" as \"no containers.\" | Knative serverless workloads are containers on Pods; the serverless property is the lifecycle (scale to zero, request-driven), not the absence of containers. | [inferred] |\n598\t| 85 | Reciting twelve-factor as twelve unrelated rules. | The factors cluster: III/IV are configuration and dependency externalization; VI/VIII/IX are the stateless-disposable-scale-out triad Kubernetes assumes; XI is why log aggregation works; X is why containers exist. | [inferred] |\n599\t| 86 | \"Factor III means use a config file.\" | Store config **in the environment** — this is precisely what ConfigMaps and Secrets injected as env vars implement. | [source] |\n600\t| 87 | Treating D3.2 Debugging as identical to D2.3 Troubleshooting. | The exam splits them; the split is roughly application-scope vs cluster-scope. A question tagged to one may still test the other's tooling. | [inferred] |\n601\t\n602\t### D4 — Cloud Native Architecture\n603\t\n604\t| # | Trap | Correct understanding | Tag |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c450c9f-3efb-4615-9d77-0943167fd975","uuid":"e98e247b-2852-4203-9337-fdeb7940a526","timestamp":"2026-08-31T09:53:21.926Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md","content":"| 72 | Expecting `kubectl top` to work without metrics-server. | `kubectl top` and autoscaling depend on the **resource metrics pipeline** (metrics-server) collecting from kubelets. | [source] |\n\n### D3 — Cloud Native Application Delivery\n\n| # | Trap | Correct understanding | Tag |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 73 | \"GitOps means running CI from Git.\" | GitOps is four specific principles about *desired state*: declarative, versioned and immutable, **pulled** automatically, continuously reconciled. CI is not part of the definition. | [source] |\n| 74 | Missing \"**pulled**\" — assuming a pipeline pushes to the cluster. | Principle 3 is explicit: agents **pull** desired-state declarations from the source. Push-based CD is not GitOps. | [source] |\n| 75 | Treating reconciliation as one-shot at deploy time. | Principle 4 is **continuous** observation and application, indefinitely. | [source] |\n| 76 | \"OutOfSync means the sync failed.\" | OutOfSync means **live state deviates from the Git target state** — including because a human changed something in the cluster. It is a drift signal, not an error. | [source] |\n| 77 | \"Argo CD only deploys plain YAML.\" | It supports Kustomize, Helm, Jsonnet, plain YAML/JSON directories, and custom config-management plugins. | [source] |\n| 78 | \"Argo CD can only track a branch.\" | It can track branches, tags, or a **pinned Git commit**. | [source] |\n| 79 | \"Helm is a templating engine.\" | Helm is a packaging system: chart (the package) → values (configuration) → templates (which render manifests) → **release** (an installed instance). | [source] |\n| 80 | Confusing **chart** with **release**. | One chart can be installed many times; each install is a separately-named release, independently upgradable and rollback-able. | [source] |\n| 81 | Confusing `charts/` with a chart repository. | `charts/` is a directory of **dependency** charts inside a chart. A chart repository is an **HTTP server** housing packaged charts, managed with `helm repo`. | [source] |\n| 82 | \"Knative is a Kubernetes replacement.\" | Knative is Kubernetes-based, builds on the Pod abstraction, and is implemented as **CRDs**. | [source] |\n| 83 | Confusing Knative Serving with Eventing. | Serving = HTTP-triggered autoscaling container runtime with scale-to-zero. Eventing = CloudEvents-over-HTTP **asynchronous** routing. | [source] |\n| 84 | Treating \"serverless\" as \"no containers.\" | Knative serverless workloads are containers on Pods; the serverless property is the lifecycle (scale to zero, request-driven), not the absence of containers. | [inferred] |\n| 85 | Reciting twelve-factor as twelve unrelated rules. | The factors cluster: III/IV are configuration and dependency externalization; VI/VIII/IX are the stateless-disposable-scale-out triad Kubernetes assumes; XI is why log aggregation works; X is why containers exist. | [inferred] |\n| 86 | \"Factor III means use a config file.\" | Store config **in the environment** — this is precisely what ConfigMaps and Secrets injected as env vars implement. | [source] |\n| 87 | Treating D3.2 Debugging as identical to D2.3 Troubleshooting. | The exam splits them; the split is roughly application-scope vs cluster-scope. A question tagged to one may still test the other's tooling. | [inferred] |\n\n### D4 — Cloud Native Architecture\n\n| # | Trap | Correct understanding | Tag |","numLines":25,"startLine":580,"totalLines":704}}}
