{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A7TcTN7P7TUh9gew2evWMy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://12factor.net/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"The Twelve-Factor App (Heroku / Adam Wiggins; maintained by the community)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles\", \"D3 Application Delivery\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"twelve-factor\", \"stateless-processes\", \"config-in-environment\", \"logs-as-event-streams\", \"disposability\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# The Twelve-Factor App (12factor.net)\n9\t\n10\tIn the modern era, software is commonly delivered as a service: called web apps, or software-as-a-service. The twelve-factor app is a methodology for building software-as-a-service apps that: use declarative formats for setup automation; have a clean contract with the underlying operating system, offering maximum portability between execution environments; are suitable for deployment on modern cloud platforms; minimize divergence between development and production, enabling continuous deployment for maximum agility; and can scale up without significant changes to tooling, architecture, or development practices. The twelve-factor methodology can be applied to apps written in any programming language, and which use any combination of backing services.\n11\t\n12\tI. Codebase — One codebase tracked in revision control, many deploys\n13\tII. Dependencies — Explicitly declare and isolate dependencies\n14\tIII. Config — Store config in the environment\n15\tIV. Backing services — Treat backing services as attached resources\n16\tV. Build, release, run — Strictly separate build and run stages\n17\tVI. Processes — Execute the app as one or more stateless processes\n18\tVII. Port binding — Export services via port binding\n19\tVIII. Concurrency — Scale out via the process model\n20\tIX. Disposability — Maximize robustness with fast startup and graceful shutdown\n21\tX. Dev/prod parity — Keep development, staging, and production as similar as possible\n22\tXI. Logs — Treat logs as event streams\n23\tXII. Admin processes — Run admin/management tasks as one-off processes\n24\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c450c9f-3efb-4615-9d77-0943167fd975","uuid":"b506dbbd-eeb8-495b-9b70-74338eb0f9fe","timestamp":"2026-08-31T09:54:10.778Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\twelve-factor-app-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://12factor.net/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"The Twelve-Factor App (Heroku / Adam Wiggins; maintained by the community)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles\", \"D3 Application Delivery\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"twelve-factor\", \"stateless-processes\", \"config-in-environment\", \"logs-as-event-streams\", \"disposability\"]\n---\n# The Twelve-Factor App (12factor.net)\n\nIn the modern era, software is commonly delivered as a service: called web apps, or software-as-a-service. The twelve-factor app is a methodology for building software-as-a-service apps that: use declarative formats for setup automation; have a clean contract with the underlying operating system, offering maximum portability between execution environments; are suitable for deployment on modern cloud platforms; minimize divergence between development and production, enabling continuous deployment for maximum agility; and can scale up without significant changes to tooling, architecture, or development practices. The twelve-factor methodology can be applied to apps written in any programming language, and which use any combination of backing services.\n\nI. Codebase — One codebase tracked in revision control, many deploys\nII. Dependencies — Explicitly declare and isolate dependencies\nIII. Config — Store config in the environment\nIV. Backing services — Treat backing services as attached resources\nV. Build, release, run — Strictly separate build and run stages\nVI. Processes — Execute the app as one or more stateless processes\nVII. Port binding — Export services via port binding\nVIII. Concurrency — Scale out via the process model\nIX. Disposability — Maximize robustness with fast startup and graceful shutdown\nX. Dev/prod parity — Keep development, staging, and production as similar as possible\nXI. Logs — Treat logs as event streams\nXII. Admin processes — Run admin/management tasks as one-off processes\n","numLines":24,"startLine":1,"totalLines":24}}}
